# checks/entry-points.allow — the reviewed list of every environment or argument read in the crates the
# shipped bridge links from outside the workspace.
#
# The ONLY list checks/entry-points.sh compares against (NFDR QC-30, half 2). The script finds the reads;
# this file says which are accepted and what each changes. See the script's header for what it reads and
# what it does not hold.
#
#   Each entry has three fields: crate | read name | what the read changes in the shipped process.
#   Write the crate name without a version; all resolved versions share one pair. Write one of these
#   nine names in full: env::var, env::var_os, env::vars, env::vars_os, env::args, env::args_os,
#   getenv, environ, _NSGetEnviron. The explanation is non-empty and may contain |. Fields are
#   trimmed. Blank lines and lines whose first non-blank character is # are ignored. A missing
#   field, an unknown name or a repeated well-formed crate and read-name pair stops the scan with
#   the list line's number. Such a line authorizes no pair. The list contains at least one entry. An
#   unlisted pair and a listed pair absent from every resolved version both stop the scan from
#   passing. If a crate cannot be read, its absent pairs are not also reported as stale. Correct the
#   numbered line or review the reported pair against the locked sources before changing the list.
#
#   Review every occurrence contributing to each added pair at the locked package version. State
#   what it changes in the shipped process. Distinguish reached reads from declarations,
#   documentation, tests, build-only code, uncompiled code and dormant calls. A statement that an
#   occurrence changes nothing needs source evidence too. Record the locked package version, the
#   matching source sites, the build conditions and the callers supporting its reach beside the
#   explanation. Evidence for one occurrence does not cover the other occurrences sharing its pair.
#   The scan checks that an explanation is non-empty; a person reviewing the list addition decides
#   whether the read is acceptable. A green comparison does not make that decision. Review existing
#   pairs and explanations again after a dependency or feature change, because a new site inside a
#   listed pair does not create an unlisted finding.

git2 | env::var | The occurrence is an environment-lookup example in a documentation comment for remote callbacks. It is not a call made by the shipped process, so it changes nothing in that process. The Git dependency's C environment lookups have their own reviewed entry.
libz-sys | env::var | zng/cc.rs only: the zlib-ng source build's `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS`, `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH`, `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_FEATURE` and `TARGET` reads, which `build.rs` compiles only under the `zlib-ng-no-cmake-experimental-community-maintained` feature (`build.rs:L175-L186`); the lock resolves only `libc` for libz-sys, so the file is not compiled and the shipped process reads nothing
libz-sys | env::var_os | zng/cc.rs only: `OUT_DIR` at `:L105`, read by the same zlib-ng source build, behind the same feature the lock does not resolve; a build-script input and not part of the shipped process
pulldown-cmark | env::args | src/main.rs:L69 only, at the locked version 0.13.6: the command-line program's `env::args().collect()` in `main`. The program is the crate's `[[bin]]` target, which `Cargo.toml:L60` gives `required-features = ["getopts"]`; `ka-reader-core` adds the crate with default features off and the lock resolves no `getopts`, so the file is not compiled and the shipped process reads no argument through it. No other file under `src/` names `env::`, so the library, which is all the core calls, reads nothing
quote | env::var_os | README.md only: a build.rs usage example in the documentation (`env::var_os("OUT_DIR")`); not compiled, so the shipped process reads nothing
syn | env::args | src/file.rs only: a doc comment's example program (`env::args()`); documentation, so the shipped process reads nothing
syn | env::var | tests/repo/mod.rs only: syn's own test helper reads ABORT_AFTER_FAILURE and RUST_MIN_STACK; a test, not part of the library, so the shipped process reads nothing

